          {
            type: "notes",
            title: "Key points",
            body: "Functions of money:\n- Unit of account: money measures value, so you can compare the price of two things.\n- Means (medium) of exchange: people accept money for goods and services, so you don't need to barter.\n- Store of value: you can save money now and spend it later. Inflation weakens this because prices rise and the same money buys less.\n- Legal tender: coins and notes that must be accepted to settle a debt. It has a narrow legal meaning. A shop can still refuse a £50 note.\n\nWhat affects the role of money in someone's life:\n- Attitude to risk and reward: a risk-averse person keeps money in safe savings. A risk-taker might buy shares for a higher possible return.\n- Attitudes to borrowing, spending and saving.\n- Life stage (see below).\n- Culture, including religious and ethical beliefs. For example, Islamic finance does not allow interest (riba), so Sharia-compliant mortgages and accounts work differently. Ethical investors avoid some industries.\n- Life events: marriage, divorce, children, illness, redundancy, inheritance, moving house. These vary the life cycle from person to person.\n- External influences and trends: inflation, unemployment, tax and benefit changes, the move to cashless payment.\n- Interest rates: the cost of borrowing against the reward for saving. When rates rise, loans, mortgages and credit cards cost more and savers earn more.\n\nLife stages:\n- Childhood: pocket money, a children's savings account, learning money habits.\n- Adolescence: part-time job, first current account, phone contract, saving for things.\n- Young adult: student loan, renting, first full-time job, building a credit history, saving for a house deposit, car costs.\n- Middle age: mortgage, family costs, usually peak earnings, paying into a pension, life insurance.\n- Old age: retirement, living on pensions and savings, care costs, making a will.\n\nWhy plan expenditure (spec list): avoid debt; control costs; avoid legal action and repossession; remain solvent (able to pay debts when due); keep a good credit rating; avoid bankruptcy; manage money to fund purchases; generate income and savings; set financial targets and goals; provide insurance against loss or illness; counter the effects of inflation.\n\nA budget compares income with expenditure. Income higher than spending is a surplus. Spending higher than income is a deficit.\n\nCredit rating (credit score): a record of how you borrow and repay, kept by credit reference agencies such as Experian, Equifax and TransUnion. Lenders use it to decide whether to lend and at what rate. To keep a good rating: register on the electoral roll, pay bills and loans on time, avoid county court judgements and bankruptcy, don't apply for lots of credit at once, and don't move house often.\n\nReal interest rate = interest rate minus inflation. Savings at 3% when inflation is 5% lose 2% of their buying power a year."
          }
