          { id: "A1-p2", q: "State two ways an individual can maintain a good credit rating. (Past paper, January 2024)", marks: 2, answer: "1 mark each, up to 2:\n- Register on the electoral roll\n- Pay bills, loans and credit cards on time\n- Avoid county court judgements, bankruptcy or insolvency\n- Avoid high levels of debt or applying for too much credit\n- Don't move home regularly\n- Use a credit builder card" },
          { id: "A1-p3", q: "Explain two advantages to an individual of planning their expenditure. (Past paper, January 2024)", marks: 4, answer: "1 mark for each advantage and 1 for a linked development, up to 4. For example:\n- Avoid getting into debt (1), so they avoid legal action or bankruptcy and keep a good credit rating (1).\n- More likely to reach personal financial goals (1) as they have more control of spending (1).\n- Avoid overspending (1) as they are more aware of their outgoings (1).\n- More likely to have savings (1), which earn interest (1).\n- Bills are paid on time (1), so no extra charges or interest (1)." },
          { id: "A1-p4", q: "Explain how a person's financial needs are likely to change between being a young adult and middle age.", marks: 4, suggested: true, answer: "Up to 4 marks for developed points, e.g.:\n- A young adult may be renting and saving for a deposit (1), while in middle age they are more likely to be paying off a mortgage (1).\n- A young adult may have student debt and a low income (1); in middle age earnings are usually at their peak, so they can save more (1).\n- In middle age they may have children (1), so costs such as childcare rise and life insurance becomes important (1).\n- Middle age is when pension saving matters most (1), because retirement is getting closer (1)." },
          { id: "A1-p5", q: "Inflation has risen to 6% and the Bank of England has raised interest rates. Ellie has £8,000 in an easy access savings account paying 3.5% and a £140,000 variable rate mortgage. Discuss the impact of these changes on Ellie's personal finances.", marks: 6, suggested: true, answer: "Indicative content:\n- Her savings earn more interest after the rise, but 3.5% is below 6% inflation, so the real value of her £8,000 still falls by about 2.5% a year.\n- Her variable rate mortgage repayments will go up straight away, cutting her disposable income.\n- Inflation also raises the cost of everyday spending, so she is squeezed from two sides.\n- She could use some savings to overpay the mortgage, as the mortgage rate is likely higher than her savings rate, but she would lose her emergency fund.\n- She could switch savings to a better rate or look at fixing her mortgage rate to make budgeting easier.\n- She may need to revise her budget and cut non-essential spending.\n\nLevels: 5 to 6 marks for a balanced discussion applied to Ellie with accurate terms; 3 to 4 for some weighing up but imbalanced; 1 to 2 for basic description." }
