        id: "A2",
        title: "A2 Different ways to pay",
        summary: "Advantages and disadvantages of each payment method, from cash and cards to BACS, Faster Payments and CHAPS.",
        resources: [
          {
            type: "notes",
            title: "Key points",
            body: "Cash: accepted almost everywhere, no fees, easy to budget, no technology needed. But it can be lost or stolen, there's no record, and more shops are going cashless.\n\nDebit card: takes money straight from your current account. No interest, widely accepted, can be used online. You can overspend into an overdraft, and card fraud is a risk.\n\nCredit card: buy now and pay later, up to a credit limit. No interest if you pay the full balance each month. Section 75 of the Consumer Credit Act protects purchases between £100 and £30,000. Can build a credit score. But interest rates are high (often 20 to 30% APR) if you don't clear the balance, and it's easy to build up debt.\n\nCheque: a written order to your bank to pay someone. Gives a record, useful for people who don't use cards. Slow to clear, can bounce if there is not enough money, and few businesses take them.\n\nElectronic transfer (online or mobile banking): quick, available 24/7, no need to carry cash or cards, done remotely. Needs internet access and can be targeted by scams.\n\nDirect debit: you let a company take payments from your account. The amount can change (e.g. utility bills). Bills are never missed, and the Direct Debit Guarantee gives a refund if a wrong payment is taken. But you must make sure the money is there or you get charges.\n\nStanding order: you tell your bank to pay a fixed amount on a set date (e.g. rent). You stay in control, but you must change it yourself if the amount changes.\n\nPre-paid card: load money on first, then spend. You can't overspend and there's no credit check, so it's good for young people and travel. There may be fees to load or use it.\n\nMobile payment apps (Apple Pay, Google Pay, PayPal): quick and convenient because people carry their phone everywhere, and secure because they need a fingerprint, face or PIN. Need a smartphone and battery, and not every shop takes them.\n\nContactless card: tap to pay, quick for small amounts. In the UK the single payment limit is £100. If the card is stolen it can be used until it is blocked.\n\nCharge card: like a credit card but the whole balance must be paid each month. Usually an annual fee and big penalties for late payment. No interest if paid on time.\n\nStore card: credit card for one retailer. Often comes with discounts when you sign up, but interest rates are very high and you can only use it in that store.\n\nMobile banking: managing your account through an app: check balances, transfer money, set up payments.\n\nBACS (Bankers' Automated Clearing Services): takes three working days. Used for wages, direct debits and paying suppliers in bulk. Cheap, but slow.\n\nFaster Payments Service (FPS): near-instant transfers, 24/7, used for most online and mobile banking payments. Banks set limits on how much you can send.\n\nCHAPS (Clearing House Automated Payment System): same-day, high-value payments with no upper limit, e.g. buying a house. Banks charge a fee, often around £20 to £35."
          }
        ],
