          { id: "A43-p1", q: "Explain two factors an individual should consider when comparing contents insurance policies.", marks: 4, suggested: true, answer: "Up to 4 marks, e.g.:\n- The premium (1), as it must be affordable over the year (1).\n- The excess (1), as a high excess means they pay more of each claim themselves (1).\n- The limit for single high-value items (1), so valuable items like a laptop are fully covered (1).\n- Whether it is new for old (1), so items are replaced at today's price (1).\n- Extras such as accidental damage (1), which may be needed for items likely to be damaged (1)." },
          { id: "A43-p2", q: "Philip is a student who has adopted a rescue puppy with an unknown medical history. Food will cost him £58 a month. The average UK pet insurance claim is £750 and an operation can cost over £3,000. Policies: Shirevale £10 a month, accidents only, maximum £500 per condition, no vet chat, £150 excess. Ashridge £38 a month, accidents only, up to £5,000 a year with no per-condition limit, no vet chat, £80 excess. Westshire £108 a month, accidents, illness and pre-existing conditions, up to £25,000, vet nurse chat, £50 excess. Royal £49 a month, accidents and illness but not pre-existing conditions, £2,000 per condition and £10,000 a year, vet nurse chat, £200 excess. Evaluate which policy Philip should choose. (Past paper, June 2024. Figures rebuilt from the mark scheme; the Royal premium is approximate.)", marks: 12, answer: "Context: Philip is a student so money is tight; food already costs £58 a month; the puppy is a rescue so may have pre-existing conditions; average claim £750; operations £3,000+.\n\nShirevale: cheapest (£10) but accidents only, and the £500 per condition limit is below the £750 average claim. After the £150 excess he can only claim £350 per condition.\n\nAshridge: nearly 4 times Shirevale but still accidents only. £5,000 a year with no per-condition limit covers the average claim and an operation. £80 excess.\n\nWestshire: most expensive at £108 a month (£1,296 a year), hard on a student budget. But covers accidents, illness and pre-existing conditions, up to £25,000, vet chat saves consultation fees, lowest excess (£50).\n\nRoyal: under half Westshire's price. Covers accidents and illness but not pre-existing conditions. £2,000 per condition covers the average claim but not a £3,000 operation. Highest excess (£200), so many vet visits won't be worth claiming.\n\nA strong answer weighs cost against cover in Philip's situation and reaches a justified choice.\n\nLevels: 10 to 12 thorough, balanced evaluation using the figures with a supported conclusion; 7 to 9 balanced with some support; 4 to 6 some application and judgement; 1 to 3 basic description." },
          { id: "A43-p3", q: "Humaira is reviewing her home insurance. She owns few valuable items: a £1,000 laptop and a cheap phone. Royal (current): £57 a month, buildings and contents, buildings excess £100, contents excess £50, emergency call-out £45 a year extra, high-value items up to £10,000. Shirevale: £45 a month, buildings and contents, buildings excess £300, contents excess £100, emergency call-out included, high-value items up to £1,000 each. Ashridge: £32 a month, buildings only, emergency call-out £47 a year extra. Westshire: £42 a month, buildings and contents, higher excess than Royal, emergency call-out £19.95 a year extra, high-value items up to £500 each. Evaluate which policy Humaira should choose. (Past paper, January 2025. Figures rebuilt from the mark scheme; the excess figures are illustrative.)", marks: 12, answer: "Shirevale: £12 a month cheaper than Royal (£144 a year), covers buildings and contents, call-out included. Highest excesses, so she funds more of any claim. £1,000 per item covers the laptop and phone.\n\nAshridge: cheapest. £25 a month less than Royal (£300 a year). Even with call-out added it's only £431 a year. But buildings only: her belongings aren't covered after a fire or break-in.\n\nWestshire: £180 a year cheaper than Royal. With call-out £523.95 a year, £16.05 cheaper than Shirevale. £500 per item covers the phone but not the laptop. Higher excess.\n\nRoyal: most expensive. With call-out £729 a year (£60.75 a month), so like for like Shirevale is £189 cheaper. £10,000 item cover is far more than she needs, though excesses are lowest.\n\nApplication: she has few valuables so may consider buildings only, but after a fire or flood she'd need to replace beds, clothes, sofas, so contents cover is wise.\n\nLevels: 10 to 12 thorough, balanced evaluation using the figures with a supported conclusion; 7 to 9 balanced with some support; 4 to 6 some application and judgement; 1 to 3 basic description." }
